{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION TO MY FRAMING — the failure is INTERMITTENT, not a clean one-way threshold crossing.\n\nI filed this ticket saying the headroom was \"now consumed\", which implies the bridge fails from here\non. The run history refutes that:\n\n    04:30  success        <- recovered on its own\n    03:31  failure\n    02:34  failure\n    01:33  failure        <- first failure, after ELEVEN consecutive successes\n    00:59  success\n    23:24  success  (and green back through 19:23)\n\nThree failures bracketed by successes on both sides. Nothing was deployed to fix it in between —\nchange 2657 is still open and unmerged — so the bridge recovered without intervention.\n\n## Why this is in tension with the measurement\n\nThe implementing agent measured, live and read-only:\n\n    statusCategory != \"Done\"  ->  1,388     (baseline 1,050 on 2026-05-26)\n    statusCategory =  \"Done\"  ->  2,992     (baseline 1,120)\n\n1,388 is well ABOVE `_ACLI_CEILING = 1200`, and the ceiling branch raises unconditionally at\n`accumulated + len(page) >= 1200`. If the reconciler's active query really matched 1,388 issues on\nevery pass, it could not have succeeded at 04:30. So at least one of these must hold, and I do not\nknow which:\n\n- the reconciler's actual query is NARROWER than `statusCategory != \"Done\"` (a project/label/date\n  filter the measurement did not replicate), so the real working set sits nearer the ceiling and\n  crosses it only sometimes;\n- the ceiling is being hit by a DIFFERENT query than the one measured (the `Done` query under\n  `_DONE_RECENT_CAP`, whose recent-window membership varies pass to pass);\n- something about the failing window is not set-size at all.\n\n## A hypothesis I hold loosely, and want disconfirmed rather than assumed\n\nThe three failures fall inside the window in which THIS session was writing heavily to the\ntracker — roughly ten new tickets created and six closed between 01:00 and 03:30. New tickets\nenter the active set; closing moves them to Done. If the active set sits just under the ceiling,\na burst of filing could push it over and the subsequent closes bring it back.\n\nThat would explain both the burst and the recovery. It is ALSO consistent with the measurement\nbeing of a wider query than the reconciler runs. But I have not tested it, and the timing\ncorrelation is exactly the kind of coincidence that looks causal at 4am. Treat it as a lead.\n\n## What this does and does not change\n\nIt does NOT change the fix direction. Whether the crossing is permanent or intermittent, a design\nwhose capacity is coupled to total project size will keep crossing as REB grows, and partitioning\nremoves that coupling. The `created`-window partition stands on its own merits.\n\nIt DOES change one acceptance criterion. \"The Reconcile Bridge workflow returns to green on its\nhourly schedule\" is now unfalsifiable — it is green already, and was green before the fix. That AC\nshould be replaced with something the intermittency cannot satisfy by accident: for example, that\na pass completes with a working set deliberately driven ABOVE the ceiling, which is what the\npartition actually buys."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788583133663858001,"uuid":"5f7c28fc-4db5-49bd-9778-e0339d3fdcb4"}